This directory includes data and python/R code for the following paper:

Robert Hsuan-Fu Hua, Ruby Chiu-Hsing Weng (2026). "A general Bayesian two-stage rating system for inferring teams and individuals from paired comparisons."

Contents:
1. Data
  1.1 sports data: volley.csv and tennis.csv
  1.2 game data: sample.dat
2. Code
  2.1 doublesAverage.py
  2.2 glicko_doublesAverage.py
  2.3 halo2.py
  2.4 McNemar_test.R
  
=======
1. Data
=======

1.1 volley.csv and tennis.csv 

The file contains at least the following four columns: "winner1_name", "winner2_name", "loser1_name", "loser2_name".

1.2 sample.dat
Due to copyright issue, the Halo2 data (SmallTeams and LargeTeams data) used in the paper are not given here. Yet, a sample data is provided for illustration. The data format is as follows.

num_game 
num_total_players 
game_id  team1_size  team2_size  team1_score  team2_score
player_id score             -----------------------
player_id score             individual's id & score
player_id score             in 1st team of 1st game        
...                         -----------------------
player_id score             -----------------------
player_id score             individual's id & score
player_id score             in 2nd team of 1st game        
...                         -----------------------

    

See data/sample.dat for an example.


==================
2. code (python/R)
==================

2.1 doublesAverage.py
===========

<Description> 

The code is used to perform Elo and two-stage Elo for volley.csv and tennis.csv data. See Algorithm 2 in the paper.

<Usage>

  python3 doublesAverage.py data.csv value1


<Input arguments>

  "data.csv": data in use

  "value1": 0 0r 1, with 0 corresponding to long-term forecast; and 1 corresponding to rolling forecast
           
<Example>

  python3 doublesAverage.py volley.csv 0
  
<Outputs>

(1) The following are shown on the screen:

  volley
  11596
  Averagevolley_0
  Averagevolley_0/
  training size: 9998
  testing size: 1598
  errRate_elo_eloD.txt
  (Elo) K with min discrepancy in train: 60
  (Elo-D) K with min discrepancy in train: 60

  It says that there are a total of 11596 games; training set contains 9998 games and test set contains 1598 games. Some results are saved in the directory Averagevolley_0.
 
(2) Under the directory Averagevolley_0/

  - The prediction errors in left-hand side of Figure 2(a) can be found in errPlot_Elo_EloD.png.
  - The values for plotting errPlot_Elo_EloD.png can be seen in errRate_elo_eloD.txt.
  - Prediction results in Table 3(a) are also extracted from errRate_elo_eloD.txt.

(3) The other results in Figure 2 and Tables 3 & 4 can be obtained similarly by running

    python3 doublesAverage.py volley.csv 1 
    python3 doublesAverage.py tennis.csv 0
    python3 doublesAverage.py tennis.csv 1


2.2 glicko_doublesAverage.py
===========

<Description> 

  The code is used to perform Glicko and the two-stage Glicko for volley.csv and tennis.csv data. See Algorithm 3 in the paper.

<Usage>

  python3 glicko_doublesAverage.py data.csv value1 value2


<Input arguments>

  "data.csv": data in use; for instance, tennis.csv or volley.csv

  "value1": 0 0r 1, with 0 corresponding to long term forecast; and 1 corresponding to rolling forecast.
  "value2": the number of lower bound on sigma
  
<Example>

  python3 glicko_doublesAverage.py volley.csv 0 0

<Outputs>

(1) The following are shown on the screen:
  
  volley
  11596
  AVEglickoOUTPUTvolley_0_0
  AVEglickoOUTPUTvolley_0_0/
 
(2) Under the directory "AVEglickoOUTPUTvolley_0_0", there are several files, where the following two
 
  "errRate_glicko.txt"
  "errRate_glickoD.txt"
  
contain the error rates for various combinations of initial (sigma, nu) for Glicko and two-stage Glicko, respectively. Each initial sigma is combined with 5 different starting nu values. The file "errRateBest_both.txt" displays the best starting nu for each initial sigma; and the best two combinations are reported in Table 5(a). 
           
(3) The results in Table 5(a) and Table 6(a)(b) can be obtained by running

  python3 glicko_doublesAverage.py volley.csv 1 0
  python3 glicko_doublesAverage.py tennis.csv 0 0
  python3 glicko_doublesAverage.py teniis.csv 1 0

2.3 halo2.py
=================

<Description> 

  The code is used to perform the two-stage rating method for Small Teams and Large Teams data. See Algorithm 1 in the paper. 

<Usage>

  python3 halo2.py data value

<Input arguments>

  "data": data in using
  "value": 's' is for defining the team score as sum of individuals' scores, and 'a' is for defining the team score as the average score.


<Example>

  python3 halo2.py sample.dat a


<Outputs>

(1) The following are shown on the screen:

  sample
  halo_sample_aScore
  halo_sample_aScore/
  accRate_ave.txt
  accRate_sum.txt

accRate_ave.txt
   Total   Correct      Acc   Larger Win   Smaller Win
      18         6   0.3333            5             1

  Larger Win Corr   Smaller Win Corr
                0                  0

  Acc Large Win   Acc Small Win
         0.0000          0.0000
accRate_sum.txt
   Total   Correct      Acc   Larger Win   Smaller Win
      18        12   0.6667            5             1

  Larger Win Corr   Smaller Win Corr
                5                  0

  Acc Large Win   Acc Small Win
         1.0000          0.0000

(2) Under the directory halo_sample_aScore/ , it contains files as follow

  "accRate_ave.txt"
  "accRate_sum.txt"

  These files contain results for average-strength and sum-up strength approaches.

(3) If we run "python3 halo2.py smallteams s", it will give results on the left hand side of Table 1(a). 
    If we run "python3 halo2.py smallteams a", it will give results on the right hand side of Table 1(a).
    Similarly, results in Table 1(b) can be obtained using the largeteams data.
    However, due to the copyright issue, the real data are not provided here.


2.4 McNemar_test.R
=============

<Description> 

It conducts one-sided McNemar Test for comparing two rating systems.

<Usage> 

  source(McNemar_test.R)
  McNemar_elo(filename_1, filename_2)

<Input arguments> 

  filename_1: output from one rating system; e.g. Averagevolley_0/elo_test.txt
  
  filename_2: output from another rating system; e.g. Averagevolley_0/eloD_test.txt
  
  
<Example>

# in R console, do the following commands:

source(McNemar_test.R)

dat1 = read.csv('Averagevolley_0/elo_test.txt', header = TRUE)
dat2 = read.csv('Averagevolley_0/eloD_test.txt', header = TRUE)
result = McNemar_elo(dat1, dat2)
result


<Outputs>

(1) It gives the z-value for 1-sided McNemar test, and the associated p-value. This is the p-value reported in Table 3(a).

(2) The p-values in Table 3(b) and Table 4(a)(b) can be obtained similarly. For instance, the p-value in Table 3(b) is obtained by

  dat1= read.csv('Averagevolley_1/elo_test.txt', header = TRUE)
  dat2= read.csv('Averagevolley_1/eloD_test.txt', header = TRUE)
  result= McNemar_elo(dat1, dat2)
  result

(3) The p-values for Glicko vs. two-stage Glicko (in Tables 5 and 6) can be obtained using McNemar_g(.,.).


